{% sec 1, "What Is an Asset Class?", "What are the different kinds of things you can buy?" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The last note was about what a market is: buyers and sellers meeting and agreeing a price. This note is about <em>what</em> gets bought.</p>
    <p>Think of a supermarket. Everything is for sale, but it's sorted into aisles: fruit in one, bread in another, cleaning stuff in a third. Each aisle holds things that behave in a similar way. Fruit goes off quickly, tins last for years.</p>
    <p>Financial markets are sorted the same way. Each aisle is called an {% gl "asset class" %}asset class{% endgl %}: a group of things you can buy that work in a similar way, react to the same kind of news, and make you money in the same kind of way.</p>
    <p>There's one question that sorts every one of them into the right aisle:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="big-idea">
    <span class="lbl">The one question</span>
    <p>What am I actually holding? A piece of something, a loan, another country's money, raw stuff, or a contract about something else?</p>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Once you know what you're holding, you know how it can make you money and what kind of news will move it. The rest of this note goes down each aisle in turn.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

