    <p class="look"><strong>Things to notice.</strong> Some aisles pay you just for holding them: shares pay dividends, bonds pay coupons, and a currency can pay interest. Commodities and most crypto pay nothing, so the price has to rise for you to make anything.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <h3>Crypto: the newest aisle</h3>
    <p>Crypto has only been around since 2009, which is why it gets a small section rather than its own chapter. It's different from the other aisles in three ways. It trades all day, every day, including weekends, with no closing time. Most of it pays no income at all, so its price rests entirely on what the next buyer will pay. And it swings far harder than anything else in this note, as section 3 shows.</p>
    <p>One part of crypto behaves very differently: {% gl "stablecoin" %}stablecoins{% endgl %}, tokens designed to always be worth one dollar. They're less of a bet and more of a way to hold and send dollars over the internet, which is why they matter so much in places where getting real dollars is hard.</p>
    <h3>Real estate: an aisle with its own shelf</h3>
    <p>Property is an asset class too: you hold land and buildings, and you make money from rent and from the price rising. It has a whole topic of its own in this hub, so it's not covered here.</p>
    <p><a class="theme-link tone-burgundy" href="/hub/real-estate/">Real Estate</a></p>
  </div>

  <div class="lab">
    <div class="lab-head"><h3>How big is each aisle?</h3><p>Roughly how much changes hands in an average day, in US dollars.</p></div>
    <svg class="chart" id="chart-size" viewBox="0 0 640 230" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart of average daily trading: FX about 9.6 trillion dollars, US government bonds about 1 trillion, shares on all the world's exchanges about 0.7 trillion, gold about 0.36 trillion"></svg>
